<section aria-labelledby="about-tool" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pt-16">
  <article class="prose-site">
    <h2 id="about-tool" style="margin-top:0">How to print a poster on multiple pages</h2>
    <p>A home printer prints on letter or A4 paper. A poster is much bigger. To print a poster on multiple pages, you cut the picture into tiles, print one tile per sheet, and tape the sheets together. This page does the cutting for you. Pick an image, choose your paper and poster size, and download one PDF with every tile in order.</p>
    <p>Everything runs in your browser. Your picture is not uploaded. The PDF has no watermark, and there is no limit on how often you use it.</p>

    <h2>Steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Choose an image.</strong> Use a JPG or PNG. A photo from a phone works well. You can also drop a file on the preview or paste one.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick the paper.</strong> Letter, A4, Legal, Tabloid, A3 or A5, in portrait or landscape.</li>
      <li><strong>Set the poster size.</strong> Choose how many pages across, or type a width or height in inches or centimeters. The tool keeps the shape of your picture.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the preview.</strong> The grid shows where each sheet falls. The tinted strips are the overlap you will glue.</li>
      <li><strong>Download the PDF and print.</strong> In the print dialog, choose 100% or Actual size. Do not choose Fit to page. That setting shrinks every tile and the pieces will not line up.</li>
      <li><strong>Trim and glue.</strong> Trim the white margin along the solid marks. Place each sheet over its left and upper neighbor, line up the picture, and glue or tape it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Margin and overlap</h2>
    <p>Most home printers cannot print close to the paper edge. Many leave a blank border of about 0.25 inch (6 mm). The printer margin setting tells the tool to keep the picture inside that safe area, so nothing is lost.</p>
    <p>The overlap is extra picture that repeats on two neighboring sheets. It gives you a strip to glue, and it hides small errors when you line up the edges. A half inch (about 1.3 cm) is a good start. Use a larger overlap if you tape from the back. Use a smaller one if you want fewer sheets.</p>

    <h2>Worked example: a 24 x 36 inch poster</h2>
    <p>Say you have a 2:3 photo and you want a 24 x 36 inch poster. Each Letter page in portrait shows 8 x 10.5 inches of picture, after a 0.25 inch margin. With a 0.5 inch overlap, each new column adds 7.5 inches and each new row adds 10 inches.</p>
    <p>Across, you need 8 + 7.5 + 7.5 + 7.5 = 30.5 inches to cover 24 inches, so 4 columns. Down, you need 4 rows. That makes 16 sheets. On landscape Letter, the same poster takes 3 columns and 5 rows, which is 15 sheets. The tool tries any setting in a moment, so you can compare before you print.</p>

    <h2>How many sheets do you need?</h2>
