    <h2>Will my picture look sharp?</h2>
    <p>The tool shows print quality in dots per inch (dpi). It divides the pixels in your image by the width of the poster in inches. About 150 dpi looks good from a normal viewing distance. 200 dpi or more looks sharp. Below 72 dpi, the poster looks blurry up close. A phone photo of 4000 pixels across is enough for a 24 inch wide poster at more than 150 dpi.</p>
    <p>If the number is low, choose a smaller poster, or start from a larger file. A tiny web image will not look good at a large size.</p>

    <h2>Ideas for tiled posters</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Classroom maps, charts and bulletin board banners.</li>
      <li>Wall art from a family photo or a child's drawing.</li>
      <li>Party banners, birthday signs and yard signs.</li>
      <li>Sewing, quilting and woodworking templates at full size.</li>
      <li>Stencils and paint-by-number guides for murals.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For a banner, use a wide image, choose Height, and type a value that fits on one page, such as 8 inches on landscape Letter. The tool then builds a single strip of pages.</p>
